                         Patch Metadata - Gen 32/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ mid-circuit-parity-swapped-ry-rz                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current program (score 0.43) suffers from poor separation performance, particularly   │
│                          │ in worst_group_margin (0.09). The prior programs achieved much better scores (0.58) by    │
│                          │ using distinct Ising gates (XX, YY, ZZ) with parameter sharing patterns and doubled RY    │
│                          │ layers before and after the interaction block.                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The recommendation suggests inserting a mid-circuit parity-swapped RY+shared RZ layer to  │
│                          │ improve expressivity while staying within the parameter budget. The key insights from the │
│                          │ better-performing prior programs are:                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Parity-based parameter sharing**: Using ry_0 on even wires and ry_1 on odd wires (or │
│                          │ vice versa) creates efficient, generalizable patterns                                     │
│                          │ 2. **Mixed Ising interaction**: Combining XX, YY, and ZZ gates with appropriate sharing   │
│                          │ creates richer entanglement                                                               │
│                          │ 3. **Multi-layer structure**: Pre-interaction RY, interaction block, mid-circuit layer,   │
│                          │ then post-interaction RZ                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new approach:                                                                         │
│                          │ - Starts with parity-shared RY on all qubits (ry_0 for even, ry_1 for odd)                │
│                          │ - Applies a shared RZ pre-layer                                                           │
│                          │ - Uses diverse Ising gates (XX, YY, ZZ) in a pattern that maximizes coverage while        │
│                          │ sharing parameters strategically                                                          │
│                          │ - **NEW**: Adds a mid-circuit parity-swapped layer with RY (ry_1 on even, ry_0 on odd -   │
│                          │ reversed) followed by a single rz_mid on all qubits                                       │
│                          │ - Ends with post-interaction RZ                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This structure adds just ONE new distinct parameter (rz_mid) while boosting expressivity  │
│                          │ through the mid-circuit reversal, staying at 11 distinct parameters (well within budget)  │
│                          │ and improving worst-case group separation.                                                │
