╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ improved_crossover_ansatz                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The crossover inspiration program achieves a score of 0.58 with better test accuracy      │
│                          │ (0.96 vs 0.86), lower generalization gap (0.04 vs 0.14), and better worst_group_margin    │
│                          │ (0.28 vs 0.27), despite having more parameters (10 distinct vs 8 distinct). The key       │
│                          │ difference is that it uses individual parameter names for RY gates (ry_0, ry_1) instead   │
│                          │ of shared names (ry_a, ry_b), and unique names for two-qubit gates (xx_01, yy_23, zz_45,  │
│                          │ xx_67, yy_14, zz_36) instead of shared names (xx_nn, yy_shared, zz_45, zz_36).            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The current program has poor generalization (gap of 0.14) and lower test accuracy. By     │
│                          │ adopting the parameter naming scheme from the better program while keeping the overall    │
│                          │ structure, we can improve generalization and robustness. The inspiration program's key    │
│                          │ advantage is its finer-grained parameter differentiation which allows each gate pair to   │
│                          │ learn independently, leading to better separation especially on difficult samples.        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The crossover strategy:                                                                   │
│                          │ 1. Keep the RZ pre-processing layer (both use rz_pre)                                     │
│                          │ 2. Use individual RY parameters (ry_0, ry_1) instead of shared (ry_a, ry_b)               │
│                          │ 3. Use unique two-qubit gate parameters instead of shared ones                            │
│                          │ 4. Keep the RZ post-processing layer (rz_post)                                            │
│                          │ 5. Keep the same gate topology and connectivity                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This maintains parameter count efficiency while improving the model's capacity to learn   │
│                          │ distinct features for different qubit pairs.                                              │
