                         Patch Metadata - Gen 71/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ crossover_crx_cry_hybrid                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ Analysis of the three programs reveals:                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Best performer (0.59)**: The crossover inspiration program uses CRX and CRY gates    │
│                          │ instead of XX and YY gates in the two-qubit interaction layer. This achieves:             │
│                          │    - Better validation accuracy (0.97 vs 0.95)                                            │
│                          │    - Better test accuracy (0.95 vs 0.93)                                                  │
│                          │    - Better worst_group_margin (0.31 vs 0.27)                                             │
│                          │    - Better validation_margin (0.59 vs 0.56)                                              │
│                          │    - Fewer distinct parameters (10 vs 10, same)                                           │
│                          │    - Faster convergence (90 vs 90, same)                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Current program (0.57)**: Uses RY parameter sharing (even/odd) for single-qubit      │
│                          │ gates and retains XX/YY gates, resulting in worse validation and test performance.        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **Key insight**: The parametrized controlled rotation gates (CRX, CRY) with            │
│                          │ per-interaction parameters appear to be more expressive and better suited for robust      │
│                          │ separation than the Ising gates (XX, YY). The CRX/CRY combination also maintains the same │
│                          │ parameter count while improving generalization.                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ **Crossover strategy**: Take the best-performing structure from the inspiration program   │
│                          │ (CRX on [6,7] and CRY on [1,4]) and combine it with the RY parameter efficiency from the  │
│                          │ current program where beneficial. However, since the inspiration program already achieves │
│                          │ the best score with CRX/CRY gates and good parameter economy, I'll use that structure as  │
│                          │ the base, which is essentially adopting the proven best approach.                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key gates to preserve are:                                                            │
│                          │ - CRX and CRY for controlled rotations (proven better than XX/YY)                         │
│                          │ - Simplified RZ post-processing (all wires share rz_post)                                 │
│                          │ - Initial RY alternation with ry_0/ry_1 pattern                                           │
